n8n published five critical advisories for its workflow-automation server, each scored 9.4 on CVSS 4.0, and each needs an authenticated user with permission to create or modify workflows ([n8n, 2026-04-22](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-q5f4-99jv-pgg5)). On 2026-04-22 it disclosed CVE-2026-42231, prototype pollution in the webhook XML body parser that chains with the Git node's SSH operations to code execution, fixed in 1.123.32, 2.17.4 and 2.18.1 ([n8n, 2026-04-22](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-q5f4-99jv-pgg5)), and CVE-2026-42232, prototype pollution in the XML node that leads to code execution when combined with other nodes, with the same fixed versions ([n8n, 2026-04-22](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-hqr4-h3xv-9m3r)). On 2026-05-13 it added CVE-2026-44789, prototype pollution in the HTTP Request node's pagination, fixed in 1.123.43, 2.20.7 and 2.22.1 ([n8n, 2026-05-13](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-c8xv-5998-g76h)); CVE-2026-44790, a flag injection in the Git node's push operation that allows arbitrary file read ([n8n, 2026-05-13](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-57g9-58c2-xjg3)); and CVE-2026-44791, a bypass of the fix for the XML-node flaw ([n8n, 2026-05-13](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-wrwr-h859-xh2r)). The advisories list workarounds: limit workflow creation and editing to fully trusted users and disable the affected node through the `NODES_EXCLUDE` environment variable ([n8n, 2026-04-22](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-hqr4-h3xv-9m3r)).

**Exposure:** self-hosted n8n below the fixed versions where users who are not fully trusted can create or modify workflows ([n8n, 2026-04-22](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-q5f4-99jv-pgg5)).

**Detection:** workflow creation and modification events by unexpected users in n8n's audit trail, and processes the n8n server spawns outside its normal node activity.

**Defender takeaway:** run 1.123.43, 2.20.7, 2.22.1 or later, and keep workflow editing limited to trusted users, since every one of these flaws starts from that permission.
